        "### Learning Rate Experiment\n",
        "\n",
        "Three learning rates were tested:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Too Low (0.0001):** The loss decreases very slowly and remains almost constant.\n",
        "- **Good (0.01):** The loss decreases steadily and reaches the lowest value, indicating effective learning.\n",
        "- **Too High (1.0):** The loss fluctuates and remains high, showing unstable training.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusion:** A learning rate of **0.01** provides the best balance between learning speed and training stability."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qm7cJF-9n3hF"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Backpropagation and the Chain Rule\n",
        "\n",
        "**Backpropagation** computes how much each weight and bias contributes to the model's prediction error. It calculates the **gradient of the loss with respect to each parameter**, which helps the optimizer update the weights and reduce the loss.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **chain rule** is needed because a neural network has multiple connected layers. The final loss depends on earlier weights through several intermediate calculations. The chain rule allows us to calculate how a change in each parameter affects the final loss by combining the gradients through each layer.\n",
        "\n",
        "In simple terms:\n",
        "\n",
        "`Prediction → Loss → Backpropagation → Gradients → Update Weights`\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore, backpropagation uses the chain rule to efficiently calculate the gradients needed to train a neural network."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "JgQoIc9FoECz"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
